 "  },",
 "  \"rule\": \"`project` (an MCP argument on the tools, ?project=<key> on a GET twin or a brain:// resource) names one of the caller's seats; the owner may name any ACTIVE project (doorProject's own validation). Anything else is refused project_forbidden { error, project, seats, note, example? } and nothing is read. Naming a project NEVER writes the session stamp (POST /project does). The tool's `project` description is src/core.js READ_PROJECT_RULE on a read and PROJECT_RULE on a write.\",",
 "  \"default\": \"no `project`: the caller's active project (the session stamp, or a single seat: whoami's activeProject), else the FIRST project in the org's order (the projects' keys, sorted) the caller may read (a seat; the owner: an active one), then the caller's other seats — never a project the caller holds no seat on; a caller seated nowhere who is not the owner reads the org root (project null). A defaulted read that could have been scoped otherwise carries projectNote (\\\"Add `project`: one of [...]\\\"), built by the helper from the projects the caller may read (their seats; the owner: the active projects, so a seatless owner's default read names them too). whoami, initialize and start-here are unchanged; whoami's activeProject stays null for a multi-seat key.\",",
 "  \"writes\": \"the writes whose validation reads the project's set (respond, rule_question, submit_grade, publish_version, restore_version, and POST /round) validate against the project they name; the person is re-stamped for the call only when the project is NAMED or is their real active one, so a multi-seat write with no `project` still answers project_required (a defaulted read scope never files a write)\",",
 "  \"answer\": \"the answer names the project it read: updates carries project {key, name}; every other scoped JSON read that carried `project` keeps it\",",
 "  \"resources\": [",
 "   \"brain://updates\",",
 "   \"brain://missing\",",
 "   \"brain://goals\",",
 "   \"brain://gtm\",",
 "   \"brain://broadcast\",",
